  "Question": "The proposition “The sum of two acute angles must be an acute angle” is false. Which of the following can serve as a counterexample?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(50^\\circ\\) and \\(70^\\circ\\)",
    "B": "\\(20^\\circ\\) and \\(30^\\circ\\)",
    "C": "\\(30^\\circ\\) and \\(40^\\circ\\)",
    "D": "\\(10^\\circ\\) and \\(60^\\circ\\)"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "A counterexample must satisfy the hypothesis (both angles are acute) but fail to satisfy the conclusion (their sum is not acute). \\(50^\\circ+70^\\circ=120^\\circ\\), which is not acute. All the other sums are less than \\(90^\\circ\\).",
